From b483890a20f6c0efe051015a3cea9bcaa9e2f5e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:04:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] divrem: same-block div/rem pair fusion for x86-64 and
 AArch64

A div/rem couple with identical operands (the hash-table and modulo
pattern `q = x/y; r = x%y`) executed TWO hardware divides on x86-64 and
AArch64 although one divide produces both results. The i686 backend has
fused them since v3 (shared compute_i686_divrem_pairs + emitter dual
store); this commit ports the fusion to the remaining two targets and
fixes a latent soundness hole in the i686 gate:

* The shared pairing function is now target-aware (DivRemTarget):
  i686 pairs 32-bit ops, x86-64 additionally pairs 64-bit (divq/idivq),
  AArch64 pairs both widths. RISC-V never pairs: div and rem are
  separate single-uop instructions there and the msub shape would be
  three operations instead of two.

* Red-team fix (latent i686 miscompile): the pairing function accepted
  constant-RHS pairs when the caller claimed the magic-number path was
  off (-Os). The register allocator always passed `false` while the
  i686 emitter passed `optimize_for_size` - an RA model narrower than
  the emitter's fusion is exactly unsound (the RA models a tail as
  emitting nothing). Constant-RHS pairs are now excluded
  unconditionally at every level, making the RA model exact.

* x86-64 emitter: pair heads emit ONE divq/idivl and dual-store
  (quotient from %rax, remainder from %rdx), tails emit nothing; the
  store ordering handles slotless heads and dead dests; %rdx stays
  excluded from allocation in dividing functions (pre-existing rule).
  The MachInst ISel path now rejects pair heads/tails so they reach
  the classic emitter (previously the fusion never fired for
  register-homed results).

* AArch64 emitter: pair heads emit GCC's exact shape - one
  sdiv/udiv, remainder folded as msub; dead tails skip the msub
  (unlike x86's free dual-output it costs a cycle). The head's own
  result lands in x0 in both orderings.

Measured: hash-mod kernel (4M variable-divisor pairs) 26.7 ms -> 17.1 ms
(1.56x), now matching GCC on the same binary shape; constant-divisor
control unchanged. Cross-TU torture (lccc-compiled impl against a
GCC-compiled reference, 200k rounds x 5 signatures with adversarial
edge values) passes 0 failures; x86-64 regression corpus 960/0.
---
