From 02b8cdd6ff14b3a1b7899da027c74fc5e1818e63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ms178 <ms178@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:21:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] i686 peephole: fix two body-deletion miscompiles in
 block-rewrite passes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Upstream's bool-phi merge-diamond threading (41c89b2d) started emitting
constant-merge diamond shapes that two v7-era i686 peephole passes
mishandled. Both bugs delete the SAME computation on SOME predecessor
paths while keeping it on others — invisible to tests whose merged
bodies had no observable effect, and latent until the new IR shapes
arrived:

1. Pattern 8 (identical adjacent block tail-merge) deleted Y's body —
   the SURVIVING copy — along with X's duplicate.  X becomes `jmp Y`,
   so every X-side predecessor must execute Y's body through the jump;
   deleting it left the merged computation nowhere.  With both arms of
   a bool diamond storing the same constant, the store vanished and
   consumers read stale stack garbage.  The existing unit test used
   flag-dead xor pairs, so the deletion was unobservable there.  Y's
   body now survives; a new unit test pins it with an observable store.

2. else_hoist_diamonds assumed the else block's only predecessor is
   the jCC it rewrites.  A second branch can target the same
   constant-else block (the multi-arm shape `if (a) ... else if (b)
   ...` compiles to two jCCs into one shared else).  Hoisting the else
   stores above the rewritten jCC silently removes them from the other
   predecessor's path.  A reference census now vetoes the transform
   when any other branch targets the else label.

Validated: upstream bool_thread_merges now passes on i686 (and five
more corpus tests that were silent victims of the same two bugs:
adler_inline_tail, inline_return_param_phi, range_check_fold,
stencil_vectorize, tce_loop_phi_param_retarget); i686 corpus 403
passed / 76 known fails / 0 new failures; x86-64 corpus 960/0; lib
gate 1302 passed / 0 failed under -D warnings.
